> Vibe code if you want. Just don't vibe your way into production.

This project is an Aruo application baseline: a small Nuxt modular monolith with explicit boundaries, validated configuration, PostgreSQL migrations, observable HTTP requests, health probes, CI, and a production container. Read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) before using an AI coding agent.

## What it guarantees

The committed quality gate formats, lints, type-checks, tests, and production-builds the application. Server configuration and API input are validated. Errors have a stable public shape. Database changes use reviewed SQL migrations. CI also audits dependencies and builds the container.

These are foundations, not a production certification. Authentication, authorization, distributed rate limiting, telemetry export, email, file storage, background jobs, backups, and provider deployment are intentionally absent. Add only what the product needs and update `aruo.yaml` when a limitation becomes a verified capability.

## Start locally

Requirements: Node.js 22–24, npm, and Docker with Compose.

```sh
cp .env.example .env
npm install
docker compose up -d db
npm run db:migrate
npm run dev
```

`GET /api/health/live` proves the process can respond. `GET /api/health/ready` verifies configuration and PostgreSQL. Neither endpoint should contain secrets or detailed dependency errors.

## Verify a change

```sh
npm run check
npm run check:security
```

`npm run check` is deterministic and runs formatting, linting, type checking, tests, and a production build. The security audit is separate because it requires the npm advisory service; CI runs both.

## Add a feature

Start with the contract and trust boundary. Put UI in `app/`, thin HTTP adapters in `server/api/`, database definitions in `server/db/`, and browser-safe shared types in `shared/`. Introduce a service only for meaningful application logic or reuse. See [the architecture guide](docs/architecture.md).

## Deploy

Build the included Dockerfile, provide `NUXT_DATABASE_URL` and `NUXT_LOG_LEVEL` through the platform's secret/configuration system, run migrations as a separate release step, then start the container. Route liveness and readiness probes to their respective endpoints. Terminate TLS at a trusted proxy and do not expose PostgreSQL publicly.

The operator owns database backups, restore drills, deployment rollback, TLS, domain policy, capacity, alerting, and migration coordination. Transitive dependencies are not yet locked by the template renderer; review the generated `package-lock.json` after the first install and commit it before deployment. See [operations](docs/operations.md) before serving traffic.

## Security and contribution

Report vulnerabilities using [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md), not a public issue. Read [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) and run the complete gate before proposing a change.

